a decorated Navy SEAL is expected back  in court today after being acquitted of  the most serious charges in a war crimes  trial a military jury cleared Special  Operations Chief Edward Gallagher of  premeditated murder yesterday he still  faces sentencing on a charge of posing  with a militants body as Jonathan  Vigliotti reports the jury at the Naval  Base in San Diego deliberated for about  eight hours I thank God my legal team my  wife Navy SEAL Chief Edward Gallagher  says he's grateful he's been exonerated  for the most serious charges against him  he walked out of military court Tuesday  with his wife Andre up by his side I was  feeling like were finally vindicated  after being terrorized by the government  that my husband fought for for two  decades in the war on terror during  Gallagher's two-week trial seven seals  from his platoon testified that he  stabbed an Isis captive in Iraq more  than two years ago the team seen here in  an Iraqi news report was injured in an  airstrike the prosecution said Gallagher  proved his own guilt with photos and  text messages including one in which he  allegedly bragged got him with my  hunting knife but in a stunning twist  another medic who was granted immunity  told the court he killed the prisoner by  holding his thumb over his tracheal tube  until he quit breathing this should  never have gotten to a trial the way  that it did defense lawyers call the  case a mutiny led by junior seals trying  to oust a demanding chief what the Navy  needs to learn is that before they  charge somebody they do a proper  investigation  Andrea Gallagher says her husband's case  raises fundamental questions about the  military justice system I think that  this vindication I hope will be a lesson  learned to everybody that we need to  uphold innocent till proven guilty due  process and we need to afford the  benefit of the doubt to our war heroes  who we send over there to fight these  evils  Gallagher has already spent more than  200 days in prison he's not expected to  serve any more time but Tony he could be  demoted which would impact his pension  and benefits all right Jonathan thank  you very much
 

